Article 9 Where a person who has reached the age of sixteen but not the age of eighteen commits theft acts not exceeding three times, and although the amount of theft has reached the standard of "relatively large amount", if after the incident the person truthfully confesses all the theft facts and actively returns the stolen property, and falls under any of the following circumstances, it may be deemed as "the circumstances are obviously minor and the harm is not great" and shall not be deemed as a crime:
(1) The person is a deaf-mute or a blind person;
(2) The person plays a secondary or auxiliary role in joint theft, or is coerced;
(3) There are other minor circumstances.
Where a person who has reached the age of sixteen but not the age of eighteen attempts to commit theft or discontinues the theft, it may not be deemed as a crime.
Where a person who has reached the age of sixteen but not the age of eighteen steals property from his or her own family or close relatives, or steals property from other relatives who request that the matter not be pursued, it may not be treated as a crime.

Article 16 Where a juvenile offender meets the provisions of paragraph 1 of Article 72 of the Criminal Law, probation may be declared. If any of the following circumstances also exists, and the application of probation will truly not cause further harm to society, probation shall be declared:
(1) It is the first crime;
(2) The offender actively returns the stolen property or compensates the victim for economic losses;
(3) There are conditions for supervision and assistance.

Article 17 Where a juvenile offender, based on the crimes committed, may be sentenced to criminal detention or fixed-term imprisonment of not more than three years, if the offender shows good repentance and falls under any of the following circumstances, the offender shall be exempted from criminal punishment in accordance with Article 37 of the Criminal Law:
(1) The offender is a deaf-mute or a blind person;
(2) Excessive defense or excessive necessity;
(3) Preparation for a crime, discontinuation of a crime, or attempted crime;
(4) An accomplice or coerced accomplice in a joint crime;
(5) Voluntary surrender or meritorious performance after the crime;
(6) Other minor circumstances of the crime that do not require criminal punishment.
